"Automatic Space Bound Analysis for Functional Programs with Garbage Collection (https://easychair.org/publications/paper/dcnD)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://easychair.org/publications/paper/dcnD\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 34\nL0: cite0†Home cite1†EPiC Series cite2†Kalpa Publications cite3†Preprints cite4†For Authors cite5†For Editors L1: [Input: Search]cite6†Image L2: cite7†Volume •cite8†Abstracts •cite9†Preface •cite10†Authors •cite11†Keywords •cite12†BibTex Entries L3: cite13†Download PDF cite14†Open PDF in browser L4: \nL5: # Automatic Space Bound Analysis for Functional Programs with Garbage Collection\nL6: \nL7: 21 pages•Published: October 23, 2018cite15†Yue Niu and cite16†Jan Hoffmann L8: ### Abstract\nL9: This article introduces a novel system for deriving upper bounds on the heap-space requirements of functional programs with garbage collection. The space cost model is based on a perfect garbage collector that immediately deallocates memory cells when they become unreachable. Heap-space bounds are derived using type-based automatic amortized resource analysis (AARA), a template-based technique that efficiently reduces bound inference to linear programming.\nL10: The first technical contribution of the work is a new operational cost semantics that models a perfect garbage collector. The second technical contribution is an extension of AARA to take into account automatic deallocation. A key observation is that deallocation of a perfect collector can be modeled with destructive pattern matching if data structures are used in a linear way. However, the analysis uses destructive pattern matching to accurately model deallocation even if data is shared.\nL11: The soundness of the extended AARA with respect to the new cost semantics is proven in two parts via an intermediate linear cost semantics. The analysis and the cost semantics have been implemented as an extension to Resource Aware ML (RaML). An experimental evaluation shows that the system is able to derive tight symbolic heap-space bounds for common algorithms.\nL12: Often the bounds are asymptotic improvements over bounds that RaML derives without taking into account garbage collection.Keyphrases: cite17†amortized analysis , cite18†cost semantics , cite19†garbage collection , cite20†lp solving , cite21†operational semantics , cite22†resource bound analysis , cite23†static analysis , cite24†type inference , cite25†type systems In: cite26†Gilles Barthe , cite27†Geoff Sutcliffe and cite28†Margus Veanes (editors). cite7†LPAR-22.\nL13: 22nd International Conference on Logic for Programming, Artificial Intelligence and Reasoning , vol 57, pages 543-563.\nL14: Links: cite29†https://easychair.org/publications/paper/dcnD L15: cite30†https://doi.org/10.29007/xkwx†doi.org L16: BibTeX entry\nL17: \nL18:     @inproceedings{LPAR-22:Automatic_Space_Bound_Analysis,\nL19:       author    = {Yue Niu and Jan Hoffmann},\nL20:       title     = {Automatic Space Bound Analysis for Functional Programs with Garbage Collection},\nL21:       booktitle = {LPAR-22. 22nd International Conference on Logic for Programming, Artificial Intelligence and Reasoning},\nL22:       editor    = {Gilles Barthe and Geoff Sutcliffe and Margus Veanes},\nL23:       series    = {EPiC Series in Computing},\nL24:       volume    = {57},\nL25:       publisher = {EasyChair},\nL26:       bibsource = {EasyChair, https://easychair.org},\nL27:       issn      = {2398-7340},\nL28:       url       = {/publications/paper/dcnD},\nL29:       doi       = {10.29007/xkwx},\nL30:       pages     = {543-563},\nL31:       year      = {2018}}\nL32: cite13†Download PDF cite14†Open PDF in browser L33: Copyright © 2012-2026 easychair.org. All rights reserved.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBeing Lazy When It Counts | TACO Lab (https://cse.hkust.edu.hk/~parreaux/publication/flops24/)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://cse.hkust.edu.hk/~parreaux/publication/flops24/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 41\nL0: # Search\nL1: \nL2: [Input: Search...]\nL3: \nL4: # Being Lazy When It Counts\nL5: \nL6: Practical Constant-Time Memory Management for Functional Programming\nL7: \nL8: cite0†John (Chun Kit) Lam , cite1†Lionel Parreaux L9: \nL10: May 2024\nL11: \nL12: cite2†PDF†lptk.github.io cite3†Cite cite4†DOI†doi.org cite5†View on conference website†conf.researchr.org L13: ### Abstract\nL14: Functional programming (FP) lets users focus on the business logic of their applications by providing them with high-level and composable abstractions. However, both automatic memory management schemes traditionally used for FP, namely tracing garbage collection and reference counting, may introduce latencies in places that can be hard to predict, which limits the applicability of the FP paradigm.\nL15: We reevaluate the use of lazy reference counting in single-threaded functional programming with guaranteed constant-time memory management, meaning that allocation and deallocation take only a bounded and predictable amount of time. This approach does not leak memory as long as we use uniform allocation sizes. Uniform allocation sizes were previously considered impractical in the context of imperative programming, but we find it to be surprisingly suitable for FP.\nL16: Preliminary benchmark results suggest that our approach is practical, as its performance is on par with Koka’s existing state-of-the-art implementation of reference counting for FP, sometimes even outperforming it. We also evaluate the effect of different allocation sizes on application performance and suggest ways of allowing large allocation in non-mission-critical parts of the program via Koka’s effect system.\nL17: We believe this potentially opens the door to many new industrial applications of FP, such as its use in real-time embedded software. In fact, the development of a high-level domain-specific language for describing latency-critical quantum physics experiments was one of the original use cases that prompted us to initiate this work.\nL18: Type\nL19: \nL20: cite6†Conference paper L21: \nL22: Publication\nL23: \nL24: In 17th International Symposium on Functional and Logic Programming (FLOPS 2024)\nL25: \nL26: cite7†Memory Management cite8†Reference Counting cite9†Real-time Systems L27: ##### cite10†Lionel Parreaux L28: \nL29: ###### Assistant Professor\nL30: \nL31: Head of the TACO Lab research group at HKUST.\nL32: \nL33:   *   * lionel.parreaux+taco-website@gmail.com\nL34:   *   *   *   * Published with cite11†Wowchemy†wowchemy.com — the free, cite12†open source†github.com website builder that empowers creators.\nL35: \nL36: ##### Cite\nL37: \nL38:     ``\nL39: \nL40: cite3†Copy cite3†Download --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJan Hoffmann (https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~janh/)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~janh/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 33\nL0: [Input]\nL1: \nL2: cite0†about cite1†team cite2†projects cite3†publications cite4†teaching cite5†cv L3: \nL4: # Jan Hoffmann\nL5: ##### Associate Professor of Computer Science\nL6: \nL7: cite6†Image L8: \nL9: I am an Associate Professor at cite7†Carnegie Mellon†www.cmu.edu ’s cite8†Computer Science Department†www.csd.cs.cmu.edu , and a member of the cite9†Principles of Programming (PoP) group and cite10†CyLab†www.cylab.cmu.edu .\nL10: My research areas are programming languages and verification. My mission is to discover beautiful mathematical ideas that have a real-world impact, shape the way programmers think, and help to create software that is more reliable, efficient, and secure. Currently, I am working on quantitative verification, type systems, static resource analysis of programs, probabilistic programming, and programming languages for digital contracts.\nL11: Before joining Carnegie Mellon, I was an Associate Research Scientist in the cite11†FLINT†flint.cs.yale.edu group at the cite12†Department of Computer Science†www.cs.yale.edu at cite13†Yale University†www.yale.edu . Before that, I was a PhD student at cite14†LMU Munich†www.lmu.de . My advisor was cite15†Martin Hofmann†www.tcs.ifi.lmu.de .\nL12: ## news\nL13: Apr 17, 2026  | Our article cite16†LFPL: Revisited and Mechanized has been accepted at cite17†LICS 2026†lics.siglog.org . Congrats to first author cite18†Nathan Glover†csd.cmu.edu .\nL14: Feb 25, 2026  | Our article cite19†Handling Exceptions and Effects with Automatic Resource Analysis as been accepted to cite20†OOPSLA 2026†2026.splashcon.org . Congrats to cite21†Ethan Chu†gediminas19.github.io on his first first-author paper.\nL15: Feb 25, 2026  | I’m looking forward to serving on the program committee of Computer Science Logic (CSL) 2027.\nL16: Jan 1, 2026  | I’m excited to teach and design a cite22†new course on probabilistic programming languages together with cite23†Feras Saad this spring.\nL17: Nov 6, 2025  | Our article on cite24†Big Stop Semantics†arxiv.org as been accepted to cite25†POPL 2026†conf.researchr.org . I’m looking forward to seeing in at POPL in Rennes.\nL18: Oct 1, 2025  | I’m excited that the NSF selected our proposal on FMitF: Track I: Probabilistic Modeling and Analysis Tools for Intermittent Systems for an award. This is a joint project with Limin Jia, Feras Saad, and Brandon Lucia.\nL19: Aug 22, 2025  | Our article on cite26†Integrating Resource Analyses via Resource Decomposition will appear at cite27†OOPSLA 2025†2025.splashcon.org .\nL20: Aug 22, 2025  | Our article on cite24†Big Stop Se